module lib/minpack/qrfac  documents every variable
  QR factorization with column pivoting, $A P = Q R$: $Q$ orthogonal (its
  columns are perpendicular unit vectors, so it changes no lengths), $R$ upper
  triangular, $P$ a permutation of the columns. lmder factors each Jacobian
  with it; $R$ and $Q^\top f$ are all the rest of the algorithm needs.

  Column pivoting means: at stage j, of the columns not yet used, bring the
  one with the most norm left (outside the rows already fixed) to position
  j. Columns that are nearly combinations of earlier ones end up last, with
  small diagonal entries, which is how lmpar detects a rank-deficient
  Jacobian.

  The Householder loop is the hot part and has no steps inside it, so the
  story shows qrfac as one call with two phases. The lines of that loop are
  explained under `how` below.

qrfac  @7cfa59a5
  what  Factor the m x n matrix a in place as $A P = Q R$. Afterwards the
        strict upper triangle of a holds $R$'s off-diagonal entries, rdiag
        holds $R$'s diagonal, the lower trapezoid holds the Householder
        vectors that define $Q$, ipvt holds $P$ (column j of $A P$ is column
        ipvt[j] of $A$), and acnorm holds the norms of $A$'s columns.
  how   For each column j in turn: (pivot) swap in the remaining column with
        the largest norm below row j; (reflect) turn that column into the
        Householder vector $u$ of the reflection that sends it to a multiple
        of $e_j$; (apply) apply the same reflection to every later column;
        (downdate) shrink each later column's norm estimate by the entry
        just moved into row j. The estimate loses accuracy once most of the
        column's norm has been removed, so it is then recomputed from the
        column. Each of those is a loop without steps, explained below as
        one line.
  why   Householder reflections keep the factorization backward stable, and
        R carries the scaling information lmder uses: the diagonal of R
        comes out in decreasing size here
        [test "pivoting puts the diagonal of R in order of decreasing size"],
        and Q R rebuilt from the output reproduces the permuted matrix to
        rounding [test "Q R reproduces the matrix with its columns permuted by ipvt"].
  a       $A$  the matrix, factored in place; below the diagonal, column j
          ends up holding the Householder vector $u$ of stage j
  ipvt    $\pi$  the column order: column j of $A P$ is column $\pi_j$ of $A$
  rdiag   $\nu$  during the loop, the norm of each unused column below the rows
          already reduced (an estimate, kept up to date by the downdate);
          afterwards $R$'s diagonal
  acnorm  $c$  the norm of each column of $A$, before any reduction
  wa      $w$  the norm of each column when $\nu_k$ was last computed from
          scratch, the reference the downdate is judged against
  ajnorm  $\alpha$  the norm of column j from row j down, given the sign of
          its diagonal entry
  col     $v$  a copy of the part of a column enorm is asked for: Viv has no
          view of part of a column, where the Fortran passes a(j,j)
  m       $m$  the number of rows of $A$
  n       $n$  the number of columns of $A$
  pivot   whether to pivot columns; lmder always asks for pivoting
  minmn   the number of reflections, $\min(m, n)$
  j       the stage: the column being reduced, 0 to minmn - 1
  i       a row index
  ii      how far down column j a loop has gone, from row j
  kk      how far past column j a loop has gone
  kmax    the column with the most norm left, among those seen so far
  k       a column after j: first a candidate for the pivot
  k@reduce#2  the old ipvt[j], held while the two entries of ipvt swap
  k@reduce#3  a column after j, which the reflection is applied to
  temp    an entry of column j, held while two columns swap
  temp@reduce#2  $u^\top a_k / u_j$  how much of $u$ to subtract from column k
  temp@reduce#3  $A_{jk} / \nu_k$  the share of column k's remaining norm that the
          reflection moved into row j
  sum     $u^\top a_k$  the Householder vector dotted with column k, from row j down
  q       $\nu_k / w_k$  how much of column k's norm is left, compared with when it
          was last computed from the column

  norms          the norm of every column of A (acnorm), the starting estimates rdiag and wa, and ipvt = the identity order
    why            Pivoting needs each column's norm, and lmder uses these norms as its scaling $D$ on the first pass.
    run            In the first factorization the column norms are {acnorm}.
  reduce         for each column j: pivot the largest remaining column in, build its Householder reflection, apply it to the later columns and downdate their norms
    why            A Householder reflection maps one vector onto a multiple of a coordinate axis while changing no lengths. Applied to column j from row j down, it zeroes that column below the diagonal; after n of them, what is left above the diagonal is $R$, and the product of the reflections is $Q^\top$.
    $$ \begin{gathered} u = \frac{a}{\pm \lVert a \rVert} + e_j, \qquad H = I - \frac{u\, u^\top}{u_j}, \\ H a = \mp \lVert a \rVert\, e_j \end{gathered} $$  [test "Q R reproduces the matrix with its columns permuted by ipvt"]
    how            The sign is chosen so that $u_j \ge 1$: adding two numbers of the same sign loses nothing to cancellation. $H$ is its own inverse, and the column stores $u$ in place of the zeros it made.
    run            In the first factorization the pivot order is {ipvt} and $R$ has diagonal {rdiag}.
    = kmax#2       pivot: of the columns not used yet, the one with the most norm left below row j
      > Swapping it into place first puts the largest remaining piece of $A$ on the diagonal, so the diagonal of $R$ comes out in decreasing size and a nearly dependent column ends up last, with a small diagonal entry.
    = ajnorm       the length of column j from row j down, the part the reflection acts on
    = ajnorm#2     give it the sign of the diagonal entry, so that the next line adds two numbers of the same sign
    = a#3          divide the column by $\alpha$: it now has length 1 and a positive entry in row j
    = a#4          add 1 to that entry: the column is now the Householder vector $u$, with $u_j \ge 1$
      $$ u = \frac{a}{\alpha} + e_j $$
    = sum#2        the dot product of $u$ with a later column k, down from row j: one line, however long the column
    = a#5          apply the reflection to column k: subtract its component along $u$, scaled by $u_j$
      $$ a_k \gets a_k - \frac{u^\top a_k}{u_j}\, u $$  [test "Q R reproduces the matrix with its columns permuted by ipvt"]
    = rdiag#2      downdate column k's norm: the reflection moved $A_{jk}$ into row j, and lengths are preserved, so what is left below row j is the rest by Pythagoras
      $$ \nu_k \gets \nu_k \sqrt{1 - \left( A_{jk} / \nu_k \right)^2} $$
    = rdiag#3      recompute the norm from the column when the downdate has cancelled away most of it
      > The test $0.05\, (\nu_k / w_k)^2 \le \varepsilon$ fires once the estimate has fallen below about $\sqrt{20 \varepsilon} \approx 7 \times 10^{-8}$ of the norm it was last computed from; by then the subtraction under the square root has lost most of its digits.
    = rdiag#4      the diagonal entry of $R$: the reflection sends the column to $-\alpha\, e_j$
      $$ r_{jj} = -\alpha $$  [hold "the first diagonal entry of R is as large as the norm of the column pivoted first"]
